                <pb facs="0139" n="19" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg19"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">821.6363 Barco/417: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Colombia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bogotá</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-01-16T18:00:00-05:00">January 16, 1931—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>5. Last Monday, January 12, Rublee took up the Barco case. He finds royalty
                    matter to be the principal difficulty. Gulf offers 5½ percent at port while
                    Government wants 6 percent, which would be zone royalty, Minister of Industries
                    says, under the new petroleum bill. Chaux states that Congress would never
                    approve a lower royalty.</p>
                <p>Rublee is still studying the matter. President’s agents are tactfully sounding
                    out Congressmen today to find out how far the Government can go. He realizes
                    that the high cost of pipe-line construction has a direct bearing on royalty in
                    this case.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
